      "text": "1. We start with $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$.\n2. To find $f(x)$, we can write $f(x)=g(x)$ and $f^{-1}(x)=h(x)$.\n3. We can set $f(x)=28x+16$.\n4. To find the inverse of $g(x)$, we rewrite $g(x)=28x+16$ as $x=\\frac{16-28y}{28}$.\n5. Thus, we have $g^{-1}(x)=h(x)$ with $h(x)=\\frac{16-28y}{28}$.\n6. After simplification, we find $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{16-28x}{28}$.\n7. Substituting $x=16-28y$ into $f^{-1}(x)=x$, we get $f^{-1}(16-28y)=16-28y$.\n8. This implies $f^{-1}(16-28y)=f((2)(16-28y)^{-1})$.\n9. Solving the equation $f^{-1}(16-28y)=16-28y$ gives $y=\\frac{4}{7}$.\n10. The sum of the two values of $x$ is $\\frac{16-28(\\frac{4}{7})}{16+28(\\frac{4}{7})}$.\n11. Therefore, the sum of all $x$ that satisfy the equation $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{16-28(\\frac{4}{7})}{16+28(\\frac{4}{7})}}$.",
